# Trivy suppression list.
#
# Only add an entry here when the finding is BOTH:
#   1. not fixable by us (no patched release we can consume), AND
#   2. mitigated by a documented compensating control.
#
# Every entry MUST carry: reason, compensating control, and a review date.
# Full rationale: SECURITY.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Eclipse Jetty 9.4.58.v20250814 (bundled by the zookeeper:3.9.5-jre-17 base
# image, used by ZooKeeper's AdminServer and Prometheus metrics provider).
#
# Not fixable:
#   - Jetty 9.4.x is community-EOL; the fixes (9.4.60 / 9.4.63) are subscriber-
#     only and not on Maven Central, so they cannot be swapped in.
#   - Jetty 10 and 11 are also EOL - their last public releases (10.0.26 /
#     11.0.26) carry the SAME two CVEs, so a 10/11 swap gains nothing.
#   - Jetty 12 has public fixes but needs zookeeper-server + prometheus-metrics
#     recompiled from source against jakarta.servlet (see SECURITY.md).
#     Targeted upstream for the ZooKeeper 4.0 line.
#
# Compensating controls (see SECURITY.md):
#   - adminServerService.external stays false (no LoadBalancer for :8080).
#   - Optional NetworkPolicy (charts/zookeeper templates) restricts :8080 and
#     :7000 to same-namespace / explicitly selected peers.
#   - AdminServer is reachable in-cluster only; not exposed to untrusted nets.
#
# Review by: 2026-12-01 (re-check for a public 9.4.x fix or ZK 4.0 GA).
CVE-2026-2332    # HIGH   jetty-http: request smuggling via chunked ext quoted-string
CVE-2026-10050   # HIGH   jetty-security: Digest auth encoding-collision auth bypass
CVE-2024-6763    # MEDIUM jetty-http: HttpURI ambiguous authority parsing
CVE-2026-6790    # MEDIUM jetty-server: response splitting via crafted header value

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# config/rbac/default_ns_rbac.yaml (Role, single-namespace mode) and
# config/rbac/all_ns_rbac.yaml (ClusterRole, all-namespaces mode) — the
# operator's own two RBAC manifests (exactly one is applied, depending on
# whether WATCH_NAMESPACE is set - see main.go).
#
# Not fixable by narrowing without a real RBAC audit (out of scope for this
# CVE-remediation PR - a wrong verb removed here breaks reconciliation, not
# CI): every rule in both manifests uses `verbs: ["*"]` because kubebuilder/
# controller-runtime's own generated-RBAC convention for an operator is
# exactly this shape - full CRUD on the child resources (Pods/Services/
# PVCs/ConfigMaps/Secrets/ServiceAccounts, Deployments/DaemonSets/
# ReplicaSets/StatefulSets, PodDisruptionBudgets) it creates, updates and
# deletes as part of every ZookeeperCluster reconcile, plus its own
# `zookeeper.pravega.io` CRD group. None of these findings are new in this
# PR - neither file is touched by it at all.
#
# Compensating controls:
#   - default_ns_rbac.yaml is a Role, not a ClusterRole - scoped to the
#     single namespace the operator's own ServiceAccount/RoleBinding live
#     in. all_ns_rbac.yaml IS a ClusterRole (that's its whole purpose - the
#     all-namespaces watch mode) - it inherently has no namespace boundary,
#     which is what KSV-0041/-0046 (secrets access / "manage all resources")
#     are really flagging; there is no narrower shape for "watch every
#     namespace" than a ClusterRole.
#   - `zookeeper.pravega.io` resources: ["*"] only reaches this operator's
#     own CRD group (currently just ZookeeperCluster) - not a wildcard over
#     unrelated resources.
#   - KSV-0056 (services/endpoints/endpointslices/networkpolicies/ingresses
#     create/update/patch/delete/impersonate) is a false positive from the
#     verbs wildcard: the operator's own code (controllers/, pkg/) never
#     calls impersonate, and never touches endpointslices/networkpolicies/
#     ingresses at all - it only creates/updates the client/headless/admin
#     Services each ZookeeperCluster needs.
#   - secrets (KSV-0041) are read/written only for the ZK ensembles this
#     operator itself creates (TLS/auth material it generates or is handed
#     via the ZookeeperCluster spec) - not an unrelated-secrets grab.
#
# Follow-up: narrow verbs per-resource-type in a dedicated RBAC-hardening
# PR (tested against a real reconcile, not just CI) rather than guessing
# here. Review by: 2026-12-01 (same date as the Jetty entry above).
KSV-0041   # CRITICAL ClusterRole manages 'secrets' (all_ns_rbac.yaml only)
KSV-0045   # CRITICAL wildcard verb on specific resources (core + apps rule blocks, both files)
KSV-0046   # CRITICAL ClusterRole wildcard resources on zookeeper.pravega.io (all_ns_rbac.yaml only)
KSV-0056   # HIGH     broad verbs on services/endpoints/endpointslices/networkpolicies/ingresses (both files)
KSV-0112   # CRITICAL Role wildcard resources on zookeeper.pravega.io (default_ns_rbac.yaml only)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docker/Dockerfile — no USER instruction.
#
# Not fixable: intentional, matches the upstream zookeeper:3.9.5-jre-17
# image's own convention. The image must start as root so its
# /docker-entrypoint.sh can chown the mounted data/datalog/logs volumes
# (which arrive as root-owned on a fresh PVC) before dropping privileges -
# see the entrypoint's own `exec setpriv --reuid=zookeeper ...` swap (this
# PR replaces upstream's `gosu` with `setpriv`, but the root-start-then-drop
# shape is unchanged from upstream).
#
# Compensating control: the container never actually runs as root - the
# entrypoint's final `exec setpriv --reuid=zookeeper --regid=zookeeper
# --init-groups zkServer.sh ...` drops to uid/gid 1000 (zookeeper) before
# the ZK process itself starts. `docker inspect`/`kubectl exec ... id`
# confirms the running process, not the image's declared USER, actually
# owns the pid.
#
# Review by: 2026-12-01.
DS-0002    # HIGH runs as root at image build time; drops privileges at runtime via setpriv
